W: 2. There was Barney McGee from the banks of the Lee
W: There was Hogan from County Tyrone
W: There was Johnny McGurk, who was scared stiff of work
W: And a chap from Westmeath called Malone
W: There was Slugger O'Toole, who was drunk as a rule
W: And Fighting Bill Tracy from Dover
W: And your man, Mick McCann, from the banks of the Bann
W: Was the skipper of the Irish Rover.
W:
W: 3. We had five million bags of the best Sligo rags
W: We had six million barrels of stones
W: We had seven million bales of old nanny goats tails
W: We had eight million barrels of bones
W: We had nine million hogs and ten million dogs
W: eleven million barrels of porter
W: We had twelve million sides of old blind horses hides
W: In the hold of the Irish Rover.
W:
W: 4. We had sailed seven years when the measles broke out
W: And the ship lost its way in a fog
W: And that whole of a crew was reduced down to two
W: Just myself and the Captain's old dog
W: Well the ship struck a rock. Oh Lord! what a shock
W: The boat she turned right over
W: She turned nine times around then the poor old dog was drowned
W: I'm the last of The Irish Rover.
